WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.051
The price on an invoice is rarely the full cost of a public service.

2
00:00:05.051 --> 00:00:11.762
Cost analysis gathers the resources required to produce an output and assigns shared support in a way suited to the decision.

3
00:00:11.762 --> 00:00:23.066
This episode adapts Kioko and Marlowe's chapter Cost Analysis, published by University of Washington Pressbooks under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 except where otherwise noted.

4
00:00:23.066 --> 00:00:27.713
It condenses, paraphrases, and reorganizes the authors' explanatory prose.

5
00:00:27.713 --> 00:00:33.490
Third-party cases, examples, figures, tables, quotations, and linked resources are excluded.

6
00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:37.320
Direct costs can be traced to a service, project, or client.

7
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:45.491
Indirect costs support several activities, such as payroll, information technology, facilities, legal services, and supervision.

8
00:00:45.491 --> 00:00:48.427
Labor cost includes compensation beyond wages.

9
00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:54.874
Capital use, maintenance, insurance, and administration can matter even when no new check is written.

10
00:00:54.874 --> 00:00:57.455
Define the cost object before calculating.

11
00:00:57.455 --> 00:01:04.767
A permit, inspection, library hour, lane-mile, emergency response, or entire program will require different boundaries.

12
00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:12.817
State the time period and whether the purpose is fee setting, grant reimbursement, comparison, outsourcing, or long-range planning.

13
00:01:12.817 --> 00:01:18.532
Shared costs need a documented allocation base with a plausible relationship to resource use.

14
00:01:18.532 --> 00:01:25.538
Headcount, labor hours, transactions, square footage, or direct cost may each be appropriate in a particular pool.

15
00:01:25.538 --> 00:01:33.835
False precision does not improve a weak causal relationship, so sensitivity analysis should show how allocation choices affect results.

16
00:01:33.835 --> 00:01:38.513
Keep accounting cost, budget impact, and economic opportunity cost distinct.

17
00:01:38.513 --> 00:01:42.207
A resource can have no new cash payment yet still be scarce.

18
00:01:42.207 --> 00:01:49.778
Conversely, an allocated cost can appear in a full-cost model even when eliminating one service would not remove that cost.

19
00:01:49.778 --> 00:01:53.779
The question determines which cost concept belongs in the answer.

20
00:01:53.779 --> 00:01:55.686
Torrance application segment.

21
00:01:55.686 --> 00:02:12.846
This original local exercise is separate from the textbook and cites the City of Torrance Budget Documents page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six, at torrance C A dot gov slash Government slash Departments Offices slash Finance slash Budget Documents.

22
00:02:12.846 --> 00:02:19.900
For a hypothetical permit service, define the cost object as one completed permit and the period as one fiscal year.

23
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:27.562
Identify direct review labor, supervision, technology, facilities, payment processing, records work, and relevant capital use.

24
00:02:27.562 --> 00:02:31.150
Document each allocation base and show a sensitivity range.

25
00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:38.751
Then compare full cost with any proposed charge while keeping access and broader public benefit as explicit policy questions.

26
00:02:38.751 --> 00:02:46.231
Do not infer Torrance's actual fee basis from this example, and consult current adopted records before using local figures.

27
00:02:46.231 --> 00:02:49.695
Full cost is an input to policy, not an automatic price.

28
00:02:49.695 --> 00:02:55.571
A government may subsidize a service for access, equity, prevention, or community-wide benefit.

29
00:02:55.571 --> 00:03:00.024
The essential practice is to make the subsidy and its rationale visible.

30
